'# Simplex Method

'The solver itself lives in the `simplex` library. Its point, apart from
solving linear programs, is to show how Dex can work with a structured matrix
without numbering its rows and columns. The simplex tableau is a table whose
rows are indexed by "this constraint, or one of the objectives" and whose
columns are indexed by "this variable, or this constraint's slack, or the
right-hand side":

import png
import plot
import simplex

'A problem is a table of constraints over some set of variables, and an
objective. Both index sets can be anything. All variables are also constrained
to be at least zero.

'## Tests

'An impossible problem: three half-planes with nothing in common.

turns = linspace (Fin 3) 0.0 (2.0 * pi)
impossible : Fin 3 => Constraint (Fin 2) = for i:(Fin 3).
  Constraint([neg (sin turns[i]), neg (cos turns[i])], 1.0, GreaterThan)

:p simplex impossible [1.0, 1.0] Maximize
> Infeasible

'An unbounded problem.

fan = linspace (Fin 3) 0.0 1.0
open_ended : Fin 3 => Constraint (Fin 2) = for i:(Fin 3).
  Constraint([neg (sin fan[i]), neg (cos fan[i])], -0.1, LessThan)

:p simplex open_ended [1.0, 1.0] Maximize
> Unbounded

'A feasible problem: Example 3.5 in *Introduction to Linear Optimization*
by Bertsimas and Tsitsiklis.

a = [[1., 2., 2.],
     [2., 1., 2.],
     [2., 2., 1.]]
textbook : Fin 3 => Constraint (Fin 3) = for i:(Fin 3). Constraint(a[i], 20.0, LessThan)

:p simplex textbook [10.0, 12.0, 12.0] Maximize
> (Solution [4., 4., 4.])

'Its tableau has a row for each of the three constraints and for the two
objectives, and a column for each of the three variables, the three slacks,
the artificial variable and the right-hand side.

:t (build_tableau textbook [10.0, 12.0, 12.0] Maximize).table
> ((Row (Fin 3)) => (Col (Fin 3) (Fin 3)) => Float32)

'A problem that needs the first phase, because the origin is not feasible.
From [these notes](http://www.eng.uwaterloo.ca/~syde05/phase1.pdf).

a' = [[ 2., -1.,  2.],
      [ 2., -3.,  1.],
      [-1.,  1., -2.]]
b' = [4., -5., -1.]
needs_phase_1 : Fin 3 => Constraint (Fin 3) = for i:(Fin 3). Constraint(a'[i], b'[i], LessThan)

:p simplex needs_phase_1 [1., -1., 1.] Maximize
> (Solution [0., 2.8, 3.4])

'Two inequalities that together make an equality, then the same with a
duplicated constraint, in two different orders.

line : Fin 2 => Float = [1., -2.]
at_most  = Constraint(line, 0.5, LessThan)
at_least = Constraint(line, 0.5, GreaterThan)

:p simplex [at_most, at_least] [1., -1.] Minimize
> (Solution [0.5, 0.])

:p simplex [at_most, at_least, at_least] [1., -1.] Minimize
> (Solution [0.5, 0.])

:p simplex [at_least, at_most, at_least] [1., -1.] Minimize
> (Solution [0.5, 0.])

'A constraint that says nothing. (Here every point with $x + y = 1$ is
equally good.)

nothing  : Fin 2 => Float = [0.0, 0.0]
together : Fin 2 => Float = [1.0, 1.0]

:p simplex [Constraint(nothing, 0.0, LessThan), Constraint(together, 1.0, LessThan)] [1.0, 1.0] Maximize
> (Solution [1., 0.])

'## A picture

'The feasible region of five constraints is a pentagon. It is drawn here
shaded by the objective, with the solver's answer marked in red: the corner
where the objective is largest.

pentagon : Fin 5 => Constraint (Fin 2) =
  [ Constraint([-0.227978, -0.973666], -0.400822, LessThan)
  , Constraint([-0.996461, -0.08406 ], -0.34026 , LessThan)
  , Constraint([-0.387869,  0.921715],  0.466923, LessThan)
  , Constraint([ 0.756744,  0.653711],  0.905228, LessThan)
  , Constraint([ 0.855563, -0.517699],  0.368932, LessThan) ]
objective = [1.1, 1.2]

best = simplex pentagon objective Maximize
:p best
> (Solution [0.5563596, 0.7407037])

def distance(x:n=>Float, y:n=>Float) -> Float given (n|Ix) =
  sqrt $ sum for i:n. sq (x[i] - y[i])

def picture(pixels:Nat, corner:Fin 2 => Float) -> Fin pixels => Fin pixels => Color =
  for i:(Fin pixels) j:(Fin pixels).
    point = [n_to_f (ordinal i), n_to_f (ordinal j)] / n_to_f pixels
    if distance point corner < 0.015
      then [1.0, 0.0, 0.0]
      else
        if constraints_satisfied pentagon point
          then [1.0, 1.0, (dot objective point) - 0.5]
          else [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]

:html case best of
  Solution corner -> imshow $ picture 150 corner
  Infeasible      -> "infeasible"
  Unbounded       -> "unbounded"
> <html output>
